Id: utf8-variants-trailing-newline
Title: A sequence cut short by a final newline is undecodable
Input: Bytes decoded with the codec utf-8-variants that end in C0 0A, or
 in the first five bytes of a six-byte surrogate pair followed by 0A
 (ED, a byte from A0 to AF, a byte from 80 to BF, ED, a byte from B0 to
 BF, then 0A), for example ED A0 BD ED B8 0A. They are reached from
 text that ends in a newline, such as 'sch\u00c3\u00b6n \u00c0\n' and
 '\u00ed\u00a0\u00bd\u00ed\u00b8\n', and so from any such line given to
 fix_text().
Reference: The newline is read as the missing last byte of the sequence
 and is lost. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
 b'abc\xc0\n'.decode('utf-8-variants') returns 'abc\x00' and
 b'\xed\xa0\xbd\xed\xb8\n'.decode('utf-8-variants') returns
 '\U0001f60a'; with any byte after the newline, or without the newline,
 both raise UnicodeDecodeError. fix_encoding('sch\u00c3\u00b6n \u00c0\n')
 returns 'sch\xf6n \x00', fix_encoding_and_explain() gives that text
 with the plan [('encode', 'latin-1'), ('decode', 'utf-8-variants')],
 and fix_text() of it returns 'sch\xf6n '.
 fix_encoding('\u00ed\u00a0\u00bd\u00ed\u00b8\n') and fix_text() of it
 return '\U0001f60a'. In a text of several lines, a line that ends this
 way is joined to the next:
 fix_text('one \u00c3\u00a9 \u00c0\ntwo \u00c3\u00a9') returns
 'one \xe9 two \xe9'. fix_and_explain('sch\u00c3\u00b6n \u00c0\n')
 returns 'sch\xf6n ' with the plan [('encode', 'latin-1'), ('decode',
 'utf-8-variants'), ('apply', 'remove_control_chars')]. The bytes are
 met through any codec the repair tries, not only the first that can
 encode the text: fix_text('David O\u201a\u00c4\u00f4Brien\u00bf\n')
 returns "David O'Brien" through macroman. And they are met at the end
 of a piece that max_decode_length cuts:
 fix_text('Grzegorz Garc\u00c3\u00ada \u00c0\n', max_decode_length=10)
 returns 'Grzegorz Garc\xeda '.
R: Decoding fails, as it does in the reference version when anything
 other than a final newline follows, so this decoding repairs nothing and
 the newline is kept. The repair then goes on as it does for any text
 that does not decode: fix_encoding('sch\u00c3\u00b6n \u00c0\n') gives
 'sch\xf6n \xc0\n', with the mojibake before the C0 repaired by the
 fixer decode_inconsistent_utf8, so fix_encoding_and_explain() gives the
 plan 'apply decode_inconsistent_utf8'.
 fix_encoding('\u00ed\u00a0\u00bd\u00ed\u00b8\n') returns its input
 unchanged, with the plan 'none'. fix_text() gives the same two texts,
 and repairs every other line of its input as the reference version
 does: fix_text('one \u00c3\u00a9 \u00c0\ntwo \u00c3\u00a9') gives
 'one \xe9 \xc0\ntwo \xe9', on two lines. fix_and_explain() keeps the
 newline in the same way, with the plan 'apply decode_inconsistent_utf8'
 for 'sch\u00c3\u00b6n \u00c0\n', and so does fix_text() for the other
 two examples: it gives "David O'\xc4\xf4Brien\xbf\n" and
 'Grzegorz Garc\xeda \xc0\n'.
 Replaying the reference version's plan for
 such a text with apply_plan() is a failed replay, at the step decode
 utf-8-variants.
Decided: docs/adr/0004-utf-8-variants-does-not-copy-upstreams-trailing-newline-behaviour.md
